OSKit Arch — aarch64 CPU Primitives
===================================

Architecture-specific CPU operations for aarch64: interrupt
enable/disable, plus (eventually) spinlock helpers and stack-frame
construction. Mirrors the shape of `oskit/arch/x86_64/cpu.lsysl`
so the kernel can import from `oskit.arch` without caring which
target is underneath.

Implementations live in `cpu_asm.s` so we can emit bare `msr`
instructions without paying for a sysl function prologue.

    module oskit.arch

Offset from the saved SP_EL1 to the syscall return register (x0).
On aarch64 the Linux syscall ABI puts the return value in x0, and
our 272-byte exception frame stores x0 at offset 0 (`[sp, #0x000]`).

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 0
    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 272        // 0x110 — matches vectors.s save/restore

Program + server address layout. User programs execute in the
per-process L3 carve-out at VA 0x60000000..0x6008FFFF (576KB):
  code  0x60000000..0x6007FFFF  (128 pages, 512KB)
  stack 0x60080000..0x6008FFFF  (16 pages, 64KB)
The layout constants (PROG_USP, PROG_SSP, SRV_USP, SRV_SSP) live in
`prog_config.sysl` so both kernel and standalone user-program builds
can share a single source of truth without dragging cpu.lsysl's
kernel-only externs into the prog build.

Mask async exceptions (IRQ, FIQ, SError). Runs one instruction
plus the return; safe to call with interrupts already masked.

    extern arch_cli()

Unmask async exceptions.

    extern arch_sti()

Transition from EL1 to EL0 and start executing at `entry` with
`SP_EL0 = sp`. Does not return — an exception back to EL1 is the
way out. Caller must ensure the active page table maps both the
entry point and the user stack EL0-accessible.

    extern drop_to_el0(entry: i64, sp: i64)

Clean the D-cache line containing `addr` to PoU, then invalidate
the I-cache and ISB. Required after writing instruction bytes via
data stores so subsequent instruction fetch sees the new code.
Short form — fine for a single cache line (64B on A72).

    extern sync_icache_line(addr: i64)

Spinlock acquire/release (LDXR/STXR exclusive monitor). `acquire`
blocks until it wins the exclusive store and masks async exceptions
on success; `release` drops the word back to zero and unmasks.

    extern arch_spinlock_acquire(lock: *i64)
    extern arch_spinlock_release(lock: *i64)

Restore a 272-byte EL0 exception frame at SP_EL1 = `frame_sp`, then
ERET. Matches the save layout in `vectors.s`. Does not return.

    extern arch_resume_process(frame_sp: i64)

The exit trampoline every new thread initially returns into; on
aarch64 it's a simple jump into `thread_exit` from boot assembly
once the kernel defines it. Referenced by `build_stack_frame` so
that a kernel thread's entry function can simply "return" and end
up inside the exit trampoline.

    extern thread_exit: byte

Per-CPU kernel stack top pointer. On aarch64 the CPU does not auto-
swap SP_EL1 on EL0→EL1 — after an ERET the current SP_EL1 remains
the one we set before the eret. That's exactly what we want for the
next trap. So we store the value the scheduler last picked and make
`tss_set_rsp0` a no-op write that future SMP work can re-use.

    var current_kstack_top: i64 = 0

    tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)
        current_kstack_top = rsp0

Build a fake EL1→EL1 exception frame on the supervisor stack so
that `arch_resume_process` can restore it and ERET into a fresh
kernel thread. Layout matches vectors.s (272 bytes):

  [0x000] x0, x1           [0x080] x16, x17
  [0x010] x2, x3           [0x090] x18, x19
  [0x020] x4, x5           [0x0A0] x20, x21
  [0x030] x6, x7           [0x0B0] x22, x23
  [0x040] x8, x9           [0x0C0] x24, x25
  [0x050] x10, x11         [0x0D0] x26, x27
  [0x060] x12, x13         [0x0E0] x28, x29
  [0x070] x14, x15         [0x0F0] x30, SP_EL0
                           [0x100] SPSR_EL1, ELR_EL1

`x30` is pre-loaded with `thread_exit` so a thread that `ret`s
from its entry function drops into the exit trampoline. SPSR_EL1
is set to `0x5` (M=EL1h, DAIF clear) so ERET stays at EL1 with
interrupts enabled; ELR_EL1 = entry_point.

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        val frame = ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var b: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *b = byte(0)
            i += 1
        var x30_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF0))
        *x30_p = i64(&thread_exit)
        var sp0_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF8))
        *sp0_p = usp
        var spsr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x100))
        *spsr_p = i64(0x5)           // M=EL1h, DAIF clear
        var elr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x108))
        *elr_p = entry_point
        frame

Same shape as build_stack_frame but the frame targets EL0 on ERET:
SPSR_EL1 = 0 (M=EL0t, DAIF clear), SP_EL0 = usp. The exit trampoline
is NOT written to x30; user code that "returns" from its entry point
should instead issue the SYS_EXIT syscall. Caller (create_process_
suspended) handles thread_exit placement separately.

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        val frame = ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var b: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *b = byte(0)
            i += 1
        var sp0_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF8))
        *sp0_p = usp
        var spsr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x100))
        *spsr_p = i64(0)             // M=EL0t, DAIF clear
        var elr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x108))
        *elr_p = entry_point
        frame

Set up the initial context frame for a new user process. On aarch64
the per-process kernel stack is identity-mapped (lives in the
Normal RAM block so it's reachable under any PT that mirrors the
kernel's L1[1] carve-out). Build the frame directly on `kstack_top_val`
— no cross-address-space copy needed. Returns the SSP value (frame
base) the scheduler will use as SP_EL1 on resume.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64,
                             kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: u64) -> i64
        build_user_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, kstack_top_val)
        kstack_top_val - i64(STACK_FRAME_SIZE)
